WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
Jimmy sells his catch at the local market. He&nbsp;
knows that he can sell only fresh fish, so he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.860
throws the bad ones away, regardless of the time&nbsp;
it takes him to find, catch, and clean the fish.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:21.600
Jimmy knows that if he were to sell spoiled goods,&nbsp;
his customers would not return. So, instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.920
crying over stale fish, he considers them as sunk&nbsp;
costs and sells what customers actually want.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.760
In his free time, Jimmy isn't’ that rational

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:45.000
Unlike fish that don't go to the movies, Jimmy&nbsp;
does. He buys himself a ticket for the afternoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.780
show. But it turns out that a few hours before&nbsp;
the film starts, his friends want to meet up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.540
play football, Jimmy's favorite sport. Since&nbsp;
he has already spent the money on the ticket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:04.500
he declines the invitation. According to sunk&nbsp;
costs theory, he shouldn’t have — the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.080
he spent on the tickets is already gone, and&nbsp;
he won’t get it back by watching the movie.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.320
What matters is that he has a good time.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:22.320
Jimmy falls for the so-called sunk cost&nbsp;
fallacy, the strong natural fear of losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.780
what we already own and the tendency of our&nbsp;
brain to treasure all the things we possess.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:35.700
A more famous example of the fallacy is the&nbsp;
Concorde — the supersonic passenger plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:42.540
Building the aircraft proved to be very difficult&nbsp;
and expensive, but instead of shutting down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.060
project, the British and French governments&nbsp;
continued funding it even though they knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:55.020
aircraft would not have any economic benefit. They&nbsp;
argued that they had invested too much to give up.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.480
To test the sunk cost fallacy, economists&nbsp;
Hal Arkes and Catherine Blumer came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.420
an experiment using theater season tickets.&nbsp;
The regular price for the season was $15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:17.400
but some people were randomly given discounts of&nbsp;
either $7 or $2. It turned out that the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:24.420
who had paid the regular price attended more plays&nbsp;
than those who received a discount. Interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.740
the only ones who behaved rationally&nbsp;
were children under the age of 6.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:37.140
Behavioral economists suggest that there are&nbsp;
three psychological reasons for our irrationality.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:45.540
1. Loss aversion: people prefer avoiding&nbsp;
losses to acquiring equivalent gains — most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.640
avoid bets where they can win $50&nbsp;
because they could also lose $50.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:58.080
2. The desire not to appear wasteful:&nbsp;
this is why some finish their food even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.800
when they feel full or don’t&nbsp;
think it’s particularly tasty.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.840
3. To ensure we do things we&nbsp;
fear we otherwise wouldn’t:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.600
We might buy expensive gym membership to&nbsp;
make sure we actually go and work out.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.800
Assuming you are immune to the Concorde&nbsp;
fallacy and you could completely ignore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.080
all investments you have ever made into your&nbsp;
education or getting to where you are today:&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.780
What would you be doing differently&nbsp;
going forward? Can you identify any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.800
'sunk costs' that are holding you&nbsp;
back from making those changes?

